<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John C. Frémont</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a>–<a href="/years/1890">1890</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as John Charles Frémont</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John C. Frémont was an American explorer, army officer and politician whose expeditions mapped much of the American West in the 1840s. In 1846 he encouraged the Bear Flag revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> and led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california-battalion">California Battalion</a> in its conquest. He later served as a senator for California, was the first Republican candidate for president in 1856 and was a Union general in the Civil War.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-c-fremont.jpg" alt="John C. Frémont" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Charles_Fremont_Oval.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John Charles Frémont was born on 21 January 1813, the son of Charles Frémon, a French-Canadian immigrant who taught French and dancing, and Anne Beverley Whiting, who had left her wealthy and much older husband for him. The couple settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, and were never married. Frémont attended the College of Charleston for two years until he was expelled for irregular attendance. His exploration work with Joseph Nicollet brought him into contact with Senator Thomas Hart Benton of Missouri, an advocate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manifest-destiny">Manifest Destiny</a>. In 1841 he eloped with Benton's daughter Jessie, and Benton became his patron.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Frémont led five western expeditions from 1842 and became known as the Pathfinder. His strength lay in scientific documentation, reports co-written with Jessie, and maps. On the first expedition, in the summer of 1842, he explored the Wind River Range with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kit-carson">Kit Carson</a> as guide. The second, from 1843 to 1844, followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-trail">Oregon Trail</a> to the Columbia River. The third, in 1845, was an armed survey of 60 men that was secretly to become a military force if war broke out with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> in 1846 he joined American settlers rebelling against Mexican authority and took part in attacks on local Native Americans, among them the Sutter Buttes massacre. Commodore Stockton made him military governor in January 1847, but Kearny held orders giving him that authority, and Frémont was court-martialled, convicted of mutiny and insubordination, and resigned from the Army.</p><p>He led a fourth expedition in 1848 and 1849 and a fifth in 1853 and 1854, both seeking a railroad route along the 38th parallel. Gold was found on his Mariposa ranch, and in 1850 he became one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>'s first two senators. In 1856 he was the first presidential candidate of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republican-party-united-states">Republican Party</a> and lost to James Buchanan. In 1861 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> made him a major general in command of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of the West, but Lincoln relieved him for insubordination after he issued an unauthorised emancipation edict in Missouri. The Radical Democratic Party nominated him in 1864, and he withdrew in September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Frémont lost much of his wealth in the Pacific Railroad venture of 1866 and in the Panic of 1873. He served as Governor of the Arizona Territory from 1878 to 1881 and died destitute in New York City on 13 July 1890. His reputation is polarised. Some regard him as a military hero, others as a failure who defeated his own best interests, and his attacks on Native Americans, which killed men, women and children, have been described as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> genocide. The Royal Geographical Society awarded him its Patron's Medal in 1850.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John C. Frémont” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Californio leader Andrés Pico and <b>John C. Frémont</b> signed an informal capitulation near Los Angeles.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-13">13 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Encouraged by the explorer <b>John C. Frémont</b>, about 30 of them seized the town of Sonoma on 14 June and took the local commander, Mariano Guadalupe Vallejo, prisoner.</blockquote><a href="/state/california-republic">California Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Savannah, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.05,-81.1&amp;year=1863" aria-label="Savannah on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John C. Frémont"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5642 4800h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5642 4800h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.05,-81.1&amp;year=1863">What was here in 1863? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
